{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Takedown policy — extdir{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}How to have an extension corrected or removed from extdir.{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<div class="wrap page">
    <div class="page-head">
        <h1>Takedown and correction policy</h1>
        <p>
            How to get something changed or removed. This procedure exists before it is needed,
            rather than being invented after the first complaint.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
        <h2>If you maintain an indexed extension</h2>
        <p>
            You do not have to justify a removal request. If you maintain a package listed here and
            you want it gone, email <a href="mailto:{{ operator.email }}">{{ operator.email }}</a>
            with the package name. It will be removed and will not be re-added by a later crawl.
        </p>
        <p>
            You can also ask for a correction instead — a wrong category, an out-of-date description,
            a compatibility row that misreads your constraint. Corrections are usually a better
            outcome for your users than removal, but that is your call, not ours.
        </p>

        <h2>What we index, and why</h2>
        <p>
            Entries are generated from metadata you published to Packagist and from your public
            repository: package name, description, licence, version constraints and repository
            activity. We do not copy your code, we do not mirror your README, and we do not reuse
            images from your repository — those may carry separate licensing.
        </p>

        <h2>Extensions without a licence are never redistributed</h2>
        <p>
            If no open-source licence is detected, the extension is <strong>indexed and linked
            only</strong>. Nothing is mirrored, rebuilt or hosted, and no install instructions are
            published. Public visibility of a repository is not permission to redistribute it, and
            extdir treats it that way by default rather than on request.
        </p>

        <h2>Copyright and trademark complaints</h2>
        <p>To have material removed on rights grounds, send:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>the exact URL or package name concerned;</li>
            <li>what the material is and which right it infringes;</li>
            <li>a statement that you hold that right or are authorised to act for the holder;</li>
            <li>contact details for correspondence.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Contested material is taken down while the complaint is assessed, not after. If the
            complaint turns out to be unfounded, it goes back up.
        </p>

        <h2>Response times</h2>
        <p>
            extdir is run by one person in their own time, so a same-day reply is not promised.
            Maintainer removal requests are acted on within 7 days. Rights complaints are
            acknowledged within 3 days and the material is taken down on acknowledgement.
        </p>

        <h2>What happens to removed entries</h2>
        <p>
            A removed extension is delisted, not silently hidden. It disappears from search, from
            categories and from its own page, and the crawler will not restore it. A record of the
            removal and its reason is kept internally so the decision can be audited — that record is
            not published.
        </p>

        <h2>Disagreements</h2>
        <p>
            If you think a removal or a refusal was wrong, reply to the same thread and say why. The
            platform's source code is public, so how any of this works can be inspected rather than
            taken on trust.
        </p>

        <p class="small subtle" style="margin-top:2rem">
            Last updated: {{ 'now'|date('Y-m-d') }} ·
            <a href="{{ path('imprint') }}">Imprint</a> ·
            <a href="{{ path('privacy') }}">Privacy</a>
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
